  "name": "Carlos Gardel",
  "editorial_article": "Born in Toulouse, France, in 1890, Carlos Gardel migrated to Buenos Aires in 1893 where he became the most influential icon in the history of tango. He rose to prominence as a singer, actor, and composer, notably creating the tango-canción genre with his 1917 recording of Mi noche triste, a work composed by Pascual Contursi and Samuel Castriota. Over his career, he sold 70,000 records in Paris alone during a 1928 visit and performed in various Paramount Pictures films, including the 1935 production El día que me quieras. His songwriting partnership with lyricist Alfredo Le Pera resulted in classics such as Volver and Por una cabeza. Gardel remained a dominant cultural force until his 1935 death in an airplane crash in Medellín, Colombia, which triggered widespread mourning throughout Latin America. He is remembered for a wide vocal range, dramatic phrasing, and for embodying the soul of the tango style, with his likeness still appearing across Buenos Aires in public spaces and murals.",
